title: Adopt a live vanilla Codex session into c2c managed delivery
updated_at: "2026-10-14T22:11:31Z"
---
Vanilla interactive Codex sessions currently auto-register through the installed `c2c hook codex` hooks and can use hook-boundary delivery, with optional `c2c deliver wake-watch` for tmux/herdr idle nudges. There is no user-facing c2c command that takes an already-running plain `codex` session and adopts its live native thread into the managed app-server delivery path.

Expected behavior:

- An operator can run a c2c command against a live vanilla Codex registration/thread, for example `c2c codex adopt ALIAS` (final grammar may differ), and receive an explicit result.
- c2c discovers the native thread and workspace safely, refuses ambiguous or already-owned threads, and creates the same durable identity/mapping and daemon sidecar ownership used by `c2c start codex` / `c2c new codex`.
- The command must preserve the running Codex frontend and its draft, avoid starting a second app-server, and report the selected transport and delivery status accurately.
- If the live session cannot be adopted, c2c gives an actionable explanation and leaves the existing hook delivery path intact.

Acceptance coverage:

1. Start a plain interactive `codex` in tmux, let `c2c hook codex` register it, detach, and verify the registration remains addressable.
2. Adopt the live session through the new c2c command, verify shared-daemon attachment/no second listener, and deliver a message.
3. Resume the adopted identity with c2c start/resume helpers and verify the saved native thread remains selected.
4. Cover ambiguous, stale, wrong-workspace, and already-owned thread refusal paths.
5. Update CLI help, docs, feature matrix, delivery checklist, and changelog guidance.

Decision: implement this as one serial Codex lifecycle lane because native thread discovery, durable mapping, daemon sidecar ownership, registry health, tests, and public documentation share the managed Codex transport seams.

Lane decision (2026-10-15): implement serially in one worktree because live Codex thread discovery, daemon sidecar ownership, registry identity, lifecycle tests, and docs all share the managed Codex transport seams. Existing CLI verified to have no adopt/attach/configure command; current vanilla support is hook registration plus optional wake-watch only.

Decision (2026-10-15): use the explicit grammar c2c codex adopt ALIAS. Require a live codex-hook registration, discover exactly one authenticated daemon thread in the registration workspace, persist the existing managed Codex mapping/config, arm the existing daemon sidecar, and leave the vanilla frontend untouched. Reuse B364 daemon/B138 seams; refuse missing/non-Codex, ambiguous, stale, wrong-workspace, existing mapping, and already-owned thread cases. Hook delivery remains the fallback until adoption succeeds.